The '''Tapajóara Confederacy''' ({{Langx|awt|Tapyiara-eté|4=True People of the River Bend}}), also known as the '''River Alliance''' ({{Langx|kp|Paraná-Jokupy|4=}}) was a loose alliance of [[chiefdom]]s that dominated the lower [[Marañón River]] [[Marañón basin|basin]] in what is now [[Balisca]] from approximately 1250 to 1532 CE. Centered on the riverine city of [[Ybytyrá-Itá]] and later [[Tava-Paraná]], the Tapajóara emerged as the preeminent power in the Marañón basin through a combination of agricultural innovation, riverine trade networks, and military conquest.

Unlike the highland civilizations of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén|Matarrañya]], the Tapajóara did not construct monumental stone architecture. Instead, they developed a sophisticated built environment of [[W:Raised field|raised field]] agriculture, [[W:Earthwork (archaeology)|earthen mounds]], [[W:Palisade|palisaded]] settlements, and extensive [[W:Canal|canal]] systems. Their capital at Ybytyrá-Itá was one of the largest settlements in pre-colonial Balisca, with an estimated population of 30,000–50,000 at its height.

The Tapajóara are notable for their complex social hierarchy, [[Tapajóara religion|religious system]] centered on river and forest deities, [[Flower War (Tapajóara)|ritualized warfare]], and extensive  [[W:Chinampa|''Ka'a-Tywyra'']]-style floating gardens (''mbaraka-ité'') that supported their large population. Their civilization represents one of the most sophisticated examples of [[W:Tropical forest adaptation|tropical forest adaptation]] in pre-colonial [[Abyala]].

==Name==
{{Main|Name of the Tapajóara}}

The name ''Tapajóara'' derives from the [[Kurupiran-Nyengatú languages|Kurupiran]] words ''tapyia'' ("bend" or "curve") and ''-ara'' ("people of"), referring to the distinctive bend in the lower [[Marañón River]] where these societies first emerged. The self-designation ''Tapyiara-eté'' means "True People of the River Bend" or "Authentic People of the Bend."

The term was originally used to refer specifically to the inhabitants of the core region around [[Ybytyrá-Itá]] but gradually came to encompass the entire confederation. Contemporary scholars use "Tapajóara" to refer to both the ruling ethnic group and the multiethnic confederation they dominated. Alternative names include the ''Tapajós Culture'' (archaeological), the ''Lower Marañón Civilization'' (geographic), and the ''Riverine Confederation'' (descriptive term).

==Geography==
{{Main|Geography of the Tapajóara Confederation}}

[[File:Chinampas.jpg|left|thumb|A ''[[W:Chinampa|Mbaraka-ité]]'' settlement in [[Mesopotàmia]], [[Estuaria]] ({{Circa|1912}}). This area has been cultivated since 1400s ]]
The Tapajóara Confederation occupied the lower [[Marañón River]] basin near [[Marañón delta|its delta]], encompassing approximately 400,000 square kilometers at its height. The region is characterized by the Marañón River itself, the largest river by discharge in Adonia, which served as the primary transportation corridor and source of sustenance for the Confederacy. Dense [[W:Marshland|marshland]] and [[W:Swamps|swamps]] covered much of the territory, requiring specialized agricultural adaptations that the Tapajóara developed as they expanded. Annual flood cycles (''pytú-guasu'') deposited fertile silt across the lowlands but also required [[W:Flood management|flood-adapted]] settlements and infrastructure. Numerous islands in the main channel provided additional land for agriculture and settlement, while the tributary networks of the [[Paraná river|Paraná]], [[Aín river|Aín]], [[Canyada river|Canyada]], and [[Tapajós river|Tapajós]] rivers connected the core region to distant trade partners.

The Tapajóara recognized five distinct environmental zones within their territory, each with specific uses and cultural significance. The ''[[Yby-ité]]'' ("true earth") comprised high ground that was never flooded, providing sites for permanent settlements, temples, and elite residences. The ''[[W:Chinampa|Mbaraka-ité]]'' ("floating gardens") consisted of artificial islands and floating agricultural platforms used for intensive [[W:Permaculture|permaculture]]. The ''Kaá-guasu'' ("great forest") encompassed the deep forest interior, used for hunting, gathering, and as a source of sacred groves. The ''Paraná-mirim'' ("little rivers") referred to the network of small rivers and tributaries that served as transportation corridors, fishing grounds, and trade routes. Finally, the ''Yvytyrá'' ("lowlands") designated seasonally flooded areas used for raised field agriculture and grazing.

==History==
{{Main|History of the Tapajóara}}

===Origins and formation (c. 1000–1250)===

The Tapajóara emerged from earlier [[Aruapará culture|Aruapará]] chiefdoms that inhabited the lower Marañón basin during the [[Formative Period (Balisca)|Formative Period]]. Archaeological evidence suggests a gradual process of social stratification, agricultural intensification, and settlement nucleation beginning around 1000 CE. According to oral tradition recorded by early Illypnian chroniclers, the Tapajóara were founded by the semi-legendary leader '''Araú-Verá''' ("Day of Light"), who led his people to the site of Ybytyrá-Itá following a vision from the river deity [[Yara-Mirim]]. Archaeological evidence confirms the establishment of a substantial settlement at the site around 1250 CE.

===Early Confederation period (c. 1250–1400)===

The early centuries saw the gradual expansion of Tapajóara influence through trade, intermarriage, and occasional military expeditions. The core settlement at Ybytyrá-Itá grew through the construction of extensive floating gardens (''mbaraka-ité'') that supported a growing population. By 1300, the settlement had become a major trade center, exchanging forest products, ceramics, and agricultural surpluses for highland goods. The formal Confederation was established around 1320, when the chiefs of seven major settlements (''tava-eté'') agreed to a mutual defense pact and system of tribute under the leadership of the Hortelão ("Great Gardener") of Ybytyrá-Itá. This political innovation allowed for coordinated action against external threats while preserving local autonomy.

===Expansion and consolidation (1400–1487)===

Under the Hortelão '''Tibiriçá-eté''' (r. 1442–1478), the Confederation underwent rapid expansion. The War of the Three Rivers (1445–1448) brought the Paraná, Aín, and lower Tapajós basins under Tapajóara control, establishing tributary relationships with previously independent chiefdoms. This period saw the development of the distinctive Tapajóara military system, including the warrior societies (''mborayú-eté'') and the practice of flower wars (''pó-ndy'')—ritualized conflicts designed to capture prisoners for sacrifice rather than territorial conquest.

===Late period and collapse (1487–1532)===

The Great Drought of 1487 severely impacted the Confederation, causing crop failures, population displacement, and political instability. The capital was moved from Ybytyrá-Itá to Tava-Paraná following the drought, and the Confederation never fully recovered its former power. First contact with Illypnians occurred around 1500, when an Illypnian expedition entered the Marañón estuary. Initial relations were cautious but peaceful, with limited trade conducted at the river mouth. However, the introduction of Old World diseases—particularly smallpox and measles—devastated the Tapajóara population in the 1510s and 1520s. The Confederation collapsed in 1532 following the Illypnian conquest of the Marañón, when a combined military expedition and indigenous allies defeated the forces of Hortelão Tava-Verá at the Battle of Ybytyrá-Itá. Survivors fled into the forest or were incorporated into the colonial labor system.

==Political structure==
{{Main|Political structure of the Tapajóara Confederation}}

The Tapajóara was a [[W:Confederation|confederation]] of various [[W:Chiefdom|chiefdoms]] (''tava-eté'') united under a paramount chief (''Hortelão''). Unlike a [[W:Centralized empire|centralized empire]], the Confederacy preserved significant autonomy for its constituent members while extracting tribute and coordinating military action. The Hortelão (Kurupiran: "Great Gardener") served as military commander-in-chief, supreme judge, and high priest of the state religion. The title reflects the central importance of agriculture to Tapajóara identity—the ''Hortelão'' ({{Literal translation|gardener|attendant}}) was conceptualized as the "keeper" of the entire alliance. The Hortelão was selected by a council of elders (''Tavyterá'') from among the eligible male relatives of the previous Hortelão. Succession was not strictly [[W:Hereditary succession|hereditary]]; candidates were evaluated for military prowess, diplomatic skill, and religious devotion.

The Council of Tavyterá ("Assembly of Chiefs") was the deliberative body of the Confederacy, composed of the chiefs of the seven founding settlements plus representatives of later additions. The council met annually at the capital to resolve disputes, receive tribute, and plan collective activities. Below the Hortelão, the political hierarchy included the ''[[Tava-ruvichá]]'' ("Chief of the Village"), who served as local ruler of a tava-eté; the ''[[Mborayú-ruvichá]]'' ("Chief of Warriors"), who led a warrior society; and the ''[[Pajé-eté]]'' ("True Shaman"), who held religious authority and served as [[W:Diviner|diviner]].

The Confederation comprised seven major chiefdoms, each with its own capital and territory. Ybytyrá-Itá, the original capital, had an estimated population of 50,000 and served as the core settlement. Tava-Paraná, with 35,000 inhabitants, became the later capital after 1487. Aruapará was a major trading center with 25,000 people, while Ygará-Pitá, with 20,000, was known as a canoe-building center. Kaá-Verá (15,000) specialized in forest products, Mbaraka-Mirim (12,000) was an agricultural center, and Pytú-Guasu (10,000) was known for flood control expertise.

=== Military ===
{{Main|Military of the Tapajóara Confederation}}

The Tapajóara military was organized around the warrior societies and the practice of [[flower wars]]. Weapons included the [[W:Macuahuitl|war club]] (''Mborayú''), a wooden club with stone or [[W:Obsidian|obsidian]] edges; the spear (''Yvyra-potí''), a wooden shaft with bamboo or stone point; the [[W:Bow and arrow|bow and arrow]] (''Ybyrá-pytá''), a bamboo bow with reed arrows of various points; the [[W:Atlatl|atlatl]] (''Yvyra-ity''), a spear-thrower for increased range; and the [[W:Blowgun|blowgun]] (''Yvyra-çú''), used for hunting and warfare. Armor was limited to padded cotton vests (''mandiyú-eté'') and wooden [[W:Shield|shields]], suitable for the hot, humid environment.

[[Flower wars]] (''pó-ndy'', "flower battle") were ritualized conflicts fought according to strict conventions. Their purpose was to capture prisoners for sacrifice rather than to conquer territory or destroy enemies. Flower wars were typically fought during the dry season, with prearranged battlefields and limited casualties. Major settlements were surrounded by palisades of wooden stakes, often reinforced with earthworks. Moats and ditches provided additional protection. However, the Tapajóara did not construct massive stone fortifications like the highland civilizations of the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén|Matarrañya]].

==Economy==
{{Main|Economy of the Tapajóara Confederation}}

The Tapajóara economy was based on intensive raised field agriculture, riverine fishing, forest foraging, and extensive trade networks. The agricultural system centered on [[W:Chinampa|floating gardens]] (''mbaraka-ité''), artificial islands constructed from layers of vegetation, mud, and silt anchored in the river and its backwaters. These gardens were highly productive, supporting multiple harvests per year and providing the caloric foundation for the Confederation's large population.

The most important crop was [[W:Manioc|manioc]] (''Mandió''), the staple crop and primary calorie source. [[W:Maize|Maize]] (''Avatí'') served as a secondary staple with ritual importance. [[W:Sweet potato|Sweet potato]] (''Jetica'') provided supplementary food, while [[W:Cacao|cacao]] (''Cacáu'') was a luxury good that also served as currency. [[W:Cotton|Cotton]] (''Mandiyú'') was essential for textile production, and [[W:Tobacco|tobacco]] (''Petymá'') was used for ritual purposes and as a trade good.

The Marañón River provided abundant fish, turtles, and other aquatic resources. The Tapajóara developed sophisticated fishing technologies including weirs and traps (''yaká''), net fishing from canoes, turtle farming in managed pools, and seasonal fish preservation through smoking and salting. These techniques ensured a reliable protein source throughout the year.

The Tapajóara controlled a vast riverine trade network connecting the Marañón basin to distant regions. Highland civilizations provided gold, silver, obsidian, and textiles in exchange for cacao, feathers, and tropical wood. The Aruanãyará chiefdoms traded shells, salt, and dried fish for pottery and agricultural products. The Nyengatú peoples exchanged medicinal plants and rare feathers for canoes and ceramics. Cacao beans served as a form of currency for large transactions, while barter dominated everyday exchange. Each major settlement hosted weekly markets (''mbaraé'') where commoners exchanged agricultural surpluses, craft goods, and forest products. The great market at Ybytyrá-Itá was said to attract thousands of traders daily, with goods from across the Marañón basin and beyond.

==Culture and traditions==

=== Traditional religion ===
{{Main|Tapajóara religion}}

The Tapajóara practiced a complex polytheistic religion centered on the veneration of river, forest, and celestial deities, ancestor spirits, and the practice of [[W:Human sacrifice|human sacrifice]]. The principal deities included [[Tupã-eté]], the supreme deity of thunder, creation, and sky who created humanity; [[Yara-Mirim]], the river goddess of water and [[W:Fertility|fertility]] who served as protector of the [[Marañón]]; [[Caá-Yara]], the forest lord of hunting and animals who provided game; [[Yacy-eté]], the moon goddess of women and cycles who served as patron of women; [[Kuarahy]], the sun god of war and warriors who served as patron of the warrior societies; and [[Añá]], the death deity of the underworld and chaos who received sacrifices.

Ritual practices included human sacrifice, in which prisoners captured in [[flower wars]] were sacrificed to Añá and other deities, typically through heart extraction on earthen temple platforms. Nobles performed [[W:bloodletting|ritual bloodletting]] to honor the gods and maintain cosmic order. Annual pilgrimages to sacred sites along the Marañón were undertaken by devotees. [[W:Hallucinogenic|Hallucinogenic]] rituals involving the use of [[W:Ayahuasca|ayahuasca]] (''caapi'') and other plant medicines were conducted for divination and spirit communication.

The dead were buried in ceramic urns (''ygaçú'') placed in household shrines or communal cemeteries. Elite burials included grave goods such as ceramics, jewelry, and sacrificed servants. [[Tapajóara temple|Tapajóara temples]] (''ogua-eté'') were constructed on raised earthen platforms, typically 5–15 meters in height, accessed by wooden staircases. The structures themselves were built of wood, [[W:Thatch|thatch]], and [[W:Woven mats|woven mats]], with elaborately painted facades depicting deities and mythological scenes. Unlike highland civilizations, the Tapajóara did not construct stone monuments, preferring organic materials that required constant renewal—a practice with deep religious significance.

=== Art and music ===
{{Main|Tapajóara art and culture}}

[[Tapajóara ceramics]] are among the finest in pre-colonial Balisca, characterized by thin-walled, finely tempered construction; polychrome slip painting in red, black, and white; geometric and anthropomorphic designs; and elaborate effigy vessels depicting deities and animals. Cotton textiles were produced on backstrap looms, featuring geometric patterns encoding tribal and clan identity, featherwork incorporated into ceremonial garments, and dyeing with plant-based pigments including red from annatto and black from genipap.

Music played a central role in religious ceremonies and social life. Instruments included [[W:Flutes|flutes]] (''miri-ité'') made of bamboo and bone; [[W:Drums|drums]] (''tupa-eté'') of hollow logs with hide membranes; and [[W:Rattle|rattles]] (''mbaraka'') of gourds filled with seeds. [[W:Ceremonial dance|Ceremonial dances]] (''jeroky'') reenacted myths and historical events. They maintained a rich tradition of oral literature, including epic poems (''mbaé-eté'') recounting the deeds of heroes and Hortelãos; religious hymns (''purahéi'') honoring the deities; proverbs and wisdom sayings (''ñe'enga''); and historical narratives (''tembiasá'') preserving collective memory.

=== Architecture and settlements ===
{{Main|Tapajóara architecture}}

Tapajóara settlements were characterized by their adaptation to the riverine environment, with architecture primarily of wood, thatch, and earth rather than stone. The civilization recognized three types of settlements: ''Tava-eté'' ("Great Settlement"), major towns with 5,000–50,000 inhabitants; ''Ocy-mirim'' ("Small House"), villages with 500–5,000 inhabitants; and ''Ygará'' ("Canoe Place"), seasonal fishing or hunting camps.

Ybytyrá-Itá ("Stone Mountain") was the first capital of the Confederacy, located on a natural rise overlooking the Marañón. Despite its name, the site featured no stone architecture—the "stone" referred to the solid, unflooded ground that made it suitable for permanent construction. The settlement covered approximately 80 hectares at its height, with a central plaza (''okára'') for ceremonies and markets; the Hortelão's palace (''oga-ruvichá''), a large wooden structure with multiple rooms; raised earthen temple platforms; residential compounds organized by ''Ocy-eté''; and extensive floating gardens in the adjacent river and nearby lakes, marshes and ponds.

Common dwellings (''oga'') were rectangular structures of wooden posts, woven palm-frond walls, and thatched roofs. A typical household compound included a main house for sleeping and storage, a separate kitchen structure, a garden plot for vegetables and medicinal plants, and a shrine for ancestor veneration.

=== Social structure ===
{{Main|Social structure of the Tapajóara}}

Tapajóara society was highly [[W:Social stratification|stratified]], with a complex hierarchy of hereditary nobles, commoners, and [[Slavery in Balisca#Pre colonial|slaves]]. The nobility (''Aba-eté'') comprised the hereditary elite, including chiefs, priests, and warriors. Commoners (''Tavy-eté'') were free farmers, artisans, and traders who formed the majority of the population. Serfs (''Mbaé-porá'') were bound laborers tied to the land, while slaves (''Añá-irú'') were war captives who often served as sacrificial victims.

The basic unit of Tapajóara social organization was the ''[[Ocy-eté]]'' ("True House"), a corporate kin group that held [[W:Usufruct|collective rights to land, fishing waters, and other resources]]. Each Ocy-eté was responsible for constructing and maintaining sections of the floating gardens, providing labor for public works, and supplying warriors for military campaigns. Ocy-eté membership was determined by both patrilineal descent and residence, allowing for incorporation of outsiders through marriage or adoption. This flexibility enabled the Tapajóara to assimilate conquered populations while maintaining social cohesion within their society.

The Tapajóara maintained several elite warrior societies (''mborayú-eté''), membership in which was earned through battlefield valor and prisoner capture. The two most prestigious were the [[Jaguar Warriors]] (''Yaguareté-eté''), elite shock troops distinguished by jaguar-skin regalia, and the [[Eagle Warriors]] (''Urubú-eté''), scouts and skirmishers distinguished by feathered headdresses. Warrior societies played important roles in both military campaigns and religious ceremonies. Their members enjoyed elevated social status, exemption from tribute, and access to elite education.

Women held significant economic and religious roles in Tapajóara society. They were responsible for agricultural production including planting, harvesting, and processing; textile production including cotton spinning and weaving; food preparation and distribution; and market trade. Elite women could serve as priestesses (''Pajé-kuñá'') and were occasionally appointed as regents for underage ''Hortelãos''. However, political and military leadership remained predominantly male.

